    "summary": "Somalia’s Minister of Planning, Investment and Economic Development, Mahmoud Abdi Sheikh Farah (Beenebeene), received UNHCR’s Acting Representative Jing Song in Mogadishu to discuss aligning the agency’s projects with national development plans.",
    "content": "In Mogadishu on Monday, Somalia’s Minister of Planning, Investment and Economic Development, Mahmoud Abdi Sheikh Farah, held talks with Jing Song, the acting representative of the United Nations refugee agency (UNHCR) in Somalia. The meeting took place at the minister’s office and centered on strengthening the strategic partnership between his ministry and UNHCR, particularly regarding planning, coordination, monitoring, and evaluation of the agency’s programs.\n\nThe discussions aimed to ensure that UNHCR’s initiatives are closely aligned with the priorities outlined in the National Transformation Plan, Somalia’s key framework for development and state-building. Minister Farah emphasized the importance of integrating humanitarian and development efforts to achieve long-term resilience for displaced populations and host communities.\n\nJing Song, who is also the deputy representative, highlighted UNHCR’s commitment to supporting Somalia’s voluntary return and reintegration programs for refugees, as well as its continued assistance to internally displaced persons (IDPs). She stressed the need for close collaboration with the federal government to effectively deliver aid and monitor its impact.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this meeting signals a continued international focus on stabilizing Somalia, which could eventually facilitate more safe return options for those considering going back to contribute to rebuilding the country. The diaspora plays a vital role in the economy, and any improvements in planning and aid effectiveness can help create better conditions for investment and engagement.\n\nThe talks concluded with both parties agreeing to meet regularly to review progress and adapt strategies as needed. This collaboration is expected to enhance transparency and efficiency in managing humanitarian resources, which remain critical for millions of Somalis affected by conflict, climate shocks, and displacement.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/07/wasiirka-qorsheynta-oo-qaabilay-ku-simaha-wakiilka-unhcr-ee-soomaaliya/",
