    "id": "news-1788807376602-5",
    "title": "African Union Welcomes UN Vote on Fairer World Maps",
    "original_title": "Midowga Afrika oo soo dhoweeyay go’aanka Qaramada Midoobay ee muujinaya cabbirka dhabta ah ee Afrika",
    "slug": "african-union-welcomes-un-vote-on-fairer-world-maps",
    "summary": "The African Union has praised a UN General Assembly resolution endorsing map projections that reflect true continental sizes, particularly Africa. The resolution urges a shift from the widely used Mercator projection to the more accurate Equal Earth projection.",
    "content": "The African Union (AU) has expressed support for a recent United Nations General Assembly resolution that encourages the global use of map projections showing the accurate proportions of continents, especially Africa. The resolution calls for a gradual transition from the Mercator projection, which distorts landmasses near the equator, to the Equal Earth projection, which better represents the actual size of land areas.\n\nSpeaking from Addis Ababa, the AU's commission chairperson, Mahmoud Ali Youssouf, welcomed the decision, describing it as a step toward correcting a long-standing visual misrepresentation. The resolution is seen as symbolic but significant, as it challenges conventional cartography that has often made Africa appear smaller than it is.\n\nThe Mercator projection, developed in the 16th century for nautical navigation, remains widely used in classrooms and atlases. However, it severely exaggerates the size of landmasses closer to the poles, making places like Greenland appear comparable to Africa, while Africa is actually 14 times larger. The Equal Earth projection, introduced by cartographers in 2018, aims to preserve relative sizes more faithfully.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this shift holds particular relevance. Many diaspora members engage with educational materials and global media that still rely on outdated maps. A more accurate representation could foster a better understanding of Africa's true geographic and economic scale, potentially influencing perceptions in business, diplomacy, and cultural identity.\n\nThe resolution, which passed with broad international backing, is non-binding but carries moral weight. It urges countries, educational institutions, and media outlets to adopt more equitable mapping standards over time. The AU's endorsement adds regional momentum, signaling a growing push for cartographic fairness.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/07/midowga-afrika-oo-soo-dhoweeyay-goaanka-qaramada-midoobay-ee-muujinaya-cabbirka-dhabta-ah-ee-afrika/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
    "source_name": "Goobjoog",
    "original_url": "https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/07/midowga-afrika-oo-soo-dhoweeyay-goaanka-qaramada-midoobay-ee-muujinaya-cabbirka-dhabta-ah-ee-afrika/",
